00:30:10,619 --> 00:30:11,719
[SPEAKER_03]: It got me pretty busy.

641
00:30:12,939 --> 00:30:14,380
[SPEAKER_03]: Then I retired in about 1960.

642
00:30:21,410 --> 00:30:21,890
[SPEAKER_04]: today.

643
00:30:23,491 --> 00:30:25,252
[SPEAKER_04]: It was not scheduled, really.

644
00:30:26,953 --> 00:30:32,676
[SPEAKER_04]: We spent last night at Lodi, which is about 110 miles away, I guess.

645
00:30:46,103 --> 00:30:46,363
[SPEAKER_04]: Where?

646
00:30:47,644 --> 00:30:48,324
[SPEAKER_04]: Petaluma?

647
00:30:48,505 --> 00:30:49,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Petaluma.

648
00:30:49,445 --> 00:30:51,106
[SPEAKER_04]: Petaluma, California.

649
00:30:51,703 --> 00:30:51,863
[SPEAKER_04]: Huh?

650
00:30:52,143 --> 00:30:52,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Petaluma.

651
00:30:53,184 --> 00:30:53,844
[SPEAKER_04]: Petaluma.

652
00:30:54,185 --> 00:30:56,386
[SPEAKER_00]: I put an N in it, I think.

653
00:30:57,507 --> 00:31:12,657
[SPEAKER_04]: This is a little bit north of San Francisco, KOA campground here on Route 101 or something like it, on Saturday night, June 24th.

654
00:31:13,778 --> 00:31:17,400
[SPEAKER_04]: We had this interview with Louis Guisto earlier today.

655
00:31:18,981 --> 00:31:20,762
[SPEAKER_04]: It was not scheduled, really.

656
00:31:22,443 --> 00:31:28,164
[SPEAKER_04]: We spent last night at Lodi, which is about 110 miles away, I guess.

657
00:31:30,024 --> 00:31:35,625
[SPEAKER_04]: And we drove on over this morning on a lovely day.

658
00:31:36,145 --> 00:31:40,826
[SPEAKER_04]: And we thought we'd try Napa, where Guisto lives.

659
00:31:41,806 --> 00:31:47,487
[SPEAKER_04]: And if not Napa, then up to Boys Springs, where Penelli lives, or Glen Ellyn, where Mark Koenig lives.

660
00:31:50,008 --> 00:31:51,448
[SPEAKER_04]: Then we thought we'd maybe

661
00:31:52,076 --> 00:31:57,097
[SPEAKER_04]: come over here after that, or else come over here before that and go back and make the interviews later.

662
00:31:57,197 --> 00:31:59,077
[SPEAKER_04]: We weren't quite sure what the plan would be.

663
00:32:00,478 --> 00:32:07,319
[SPEAKER_04]: But we got into Napa, got out to Guisto's house about quarter of 11, and he wasn't there.

664
00:32:07,359 --> 00:32:11,720
[SPEAKER_04]: The garage was empty, and the spray was going in the front lawn.

665
00:32:12,840 --> 00:32:13,961
[SPEAKER_04]: I went up and knocked on the door.

666
00:32:14,021 --> 00:32:14,501
[SPEAKER_04]: No answer.

667
00:32:14,521 --> 00:32:20,702
[SPEAKER_04]: A gentleman from across the street came over and said that he saw we were from West Virginia, and he wanted to make sure we

668
00:32:22,316 --> 00:32:28,897
[SPEAKER_04]: knew what the story was, so he said Guisto had gone out shortly before and should be back very soon.

669
00:32:29,538 --> 00:32:36,699
[SPEAKER_04]: Even though he was in his 80s, he was in good shape physically and would be disappointed if we hadn't seen him.

670
00:32:36,719 --> 00:32:37,459
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know about that.

671
00:32:38,920 --> 00:32:40,040
[SPEAKER_04]: So we thought about it.

672
00:32:40,060 --> 00:32:41,840
[SPEAKER_04]: Then we went into town and got something to eat.

673
00:32:41,860 --> 00:32:42,660
[SPEAKER_04]: We hadn't eaten much.

674
00:32:42,700 --> 00:32:48,061
[SPEAKER_04]: We went into the butternut dairy, butterflower bakery, I guess it was.

675
00:32:48,081 --> 00:32:50,402
[SPEAKER_04]: Had some coffee and donuts.

676
00:32:52,353 --> 00:32:55,016
[SPEAKER_04]: Then came back out, and it was about 11.30 now.

677
00:32:55,036 --> 00:32:58,279
[SPEAKER_04]: And I talked to somebody else who said he should be back.

678
00:32:59,220 --> 00:33:00,301
[SPEAKER_04]: We waited out in the car.

679
00:33:00,321 --> 00:33:02,163
[SPEAKER_04]: We were going to leave after about 10 minutes.

680
00:33:02,203 --> 00:33:03,484
[SPEAKER_04]: But he pulled in about then.

681
00:33:05,105 --> 00:33:13,474
[SPEAKER_04]: And the gentleman next door came rushing out to tell him that there were some visitors from West Virginia, which didn't seem to thrill him greatly.

682
00:33:13,514 --> 00:33:16,917
[SPEAKER_04]: But he acknowledged us and invited us in.

683
00:33:18,278 --> 00:33:19,499
[SPEAKER_04]: And he was kind of rushed.

684
00:33:19,519 --> 00:33:23,981
[SPEAKER_04]: He's a very ill sister, and he had other things to do.

685
00:33:25,841 --> 00:33:27,382
[SPEAKER_04]: But he could give us a little bit of time.

686
00:33:27,502 --> 00:33:33,104
[SPEAKER_04]: So we sat down there about 11.30 and had the half-hour tape, the other side of this tape.

687
00:33:33,124 --> 00:33:36,966
[SPEAKER_04]: It wasn't much to it, but at least I talked to him and I heard him.

688
00:33:36,986 --> 00:33:40,108
[SPEAKER_04]: He signed the ball, and we got a couple pictures.

689
00:33:41,348 --> 00:33:42,709
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, could you describe the house?

690
00:33:45,614 --> 00:34:02,919
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a stone house with a little porch-like affair coming out in front, a veranda-type thing that was covered with ivy that was thicker than your arm, the stalks.

691
00:34:03,139 --> 00:34:07,801
[SPEAKER_04]: You said you could take off the porch pillars and the ivy stalks would hold up the roof.

692
00:34:08,381 --> 00:34:08,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

693
00:34:09,601 --> 00:34:12,562
[SPEAKER_00]: And inside the house was very nicely furnished, actually.

694
00:34:14,063 --> 00:34:21,188
[SPEAKER_00]: not modern by any means, but traditional furniture and pictures all over of nieces and nephews.

695
00:34:21,248 --> 00:34:25,591
[SPEAKER_00]: I think he has 15 nephews living in Napa alone.

696
00:34:26,212 --> 00:34:33,137
[SPEAKER_00]: In the dining room, it was a traditional dining room thing with a lace tablecloth on it, a plastic tablecloth on top of that.

697
00:34:33,857 --> 00:34:37,880
[SPEAKER_00]: And the sideboard or the buffet was loaded with liquor bottles.

698
00:34:39,742 --> 00:34:41,603
[SPEAKER_00]: And so were all the ashtrays loaded with

699
00:34:42,058 --> 00:34:43,719
[SPEAKER_00]: They had all been used.

700
00:34:43,819 --> 00:34:49,683
[SPEAKER_00]: And then that little kitchen or den, whatever it was, was piled high with papers and books.

701
00:34:49,883 --> 00:34:53,925
[SPEAKER_00]: It looked like your type of journal, whatever was in it.

702
00:34:53,945 --> 00:34:58,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe it does bookkeeping on the side or something.

703
00:34:58,048 --> 00:35:00,209
[SPEAKER_04]: A little bit of clutter out there in the back rooms.

704
00:35:00,849 --> 00:35:01,890
[SPEAKER_00]: But not in the living room.

705
00:35:01,930 --> 00:35:02,010
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

706
00:35:02,070 --> 00:35:05,552
[SPEAKER_00]: It was except for the pictures, which were all over Kingdom Come.

707
00:35:06,092 --> 00:35:08,574
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, they must be Catholic, because they had the

708
00:35:09,665 --> 00:35:12,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Madonna and child up there in a crucifix.

709
00:35:14,488 --> 00:35:16,309
[SPEAKER_00]: And has no children of his own.

710
00:35:16,950 --> 00:35:19,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether he was married or not, we never did find out.

711
00:35:20,412 --> 00:35:25,456
[SPEAKER_00]: But he has a, we know one younger sister and one older sister.

712
00:35:26,196 --> 00:35:30,339
[SPEAKER_00]: And the younger sister has six grandchildren or something.

713
00:35:30,519 --> 00:35:36,323
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when he said he had 15 to 18 nephews in the valley in Napa world.

714
00:35:40,060 --> 00:35:44,241
[SPEAKER_04]: The front of the house is covered with bushes and trees and shrubbery.

715
00:35:44,261 --> 00:35:45,401
[SPEAKER_04]: You could hardly see the house.

716
00:35:46,262 --> 00:35:54,804
[SPEAKER_04]: And I walked around the back where this other gentleman spoke to me, and he had looked rather cluttered in back with bushes and trees.

717
00:35:54,924 --> 00:35:56,284
[SPEAKER_04]: I didn't see any lawn or anything.

718
00:35:57,105 --> 00:36:03,766
[SPEAKER_04]: Maybe he's got an orchard and some vines of his own out there.

719
00:36:03,786 --> 00:36:07,467
[SPEAKER_00]: He's a very good health for his age.

720
00:36:07,507 --> 00:36:08,468
[SPEAKER_04]: He didn't look 83.

721
00:36:09,932 --> 00:36:12,754
[SPEAKER_04]: Little hunched over, very stout, he always was stout.

722
00:36:13,435 --> 00:36:18,059
[SPEAKER_04]: Gray-haired, rather firm features and a firm manner.

723
00:36:20,541 --> 00:36:23,443
[SPEAKER_04]: I remember his pictures back in the 20s with the mustache.

724
00:36:23,483 --> 00:36:25,885
[SPEAKER_04]: He seemed so unusual wearing a mustache then.

725
00:36:26,725 --> 00:36:27,366
[SPEAKER_04]: I think I did.

726
00:36:27,946 --> 00:36:28,947
[SPEAKER_04]: He didn't have one now.

727
00:36:29,668 --> 00:36:33,030
[SPEAKER_04]: But he still was recognizable, though, as I recall those old pictures.

728
00:36:36,113 --> 00:36:38,655
[SPEAKER_00]: But he didn't even tell any good stories after we left.

729
00:36:39,008 --> 00:36:40,388
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, after the tape went off.

730
00:36:41,649 --> 00:36:45,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I think he was surprised that you left as soon as it switched off.

731
00:36:45,910 --> 00:36:46,311
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, you do?

732
00:36:47,171 --> 00:36:47,411
[SPEAKER_04]: Why?

733
00:36:47,791 --> 00:36:48,211
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

734
00:36:48,251 --> 00:36:52,373
[SPEAKER_00]: I just got that impression that he was surprised you were ready to leave.

735
00:36:53,573 --> 00:36:55,554
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, I felt that he wanted me to leave.

736
00:36:56,734 --> 00:36:59,175
[SPEAKER_04]: It was kind of an imposition coming in like that.

737
00:37:00,695 --> 00:37:02,616
[SPEAKER_04]: And I said, do you have some time?

738
00:37:02,636 --> 00:37:03,756
[SPEAKER_04]: He said, well, a few minutes.

739
00:37:03,776 --> 00:37:05,657
[SPEAKER_04]: And I said, well, he said, how much time?

740
00:37:05,697 --> 00:37:06,477
[SPEAKER_04]: I said, maybe an hour.

741
00:37:06,497 --> 00:37:08,298
[SPEAKER_04]: And he looked at his watch and said, well, just an hour.

742
00:37:09,700 --> 00:37:14,542
[SPEAKER_04]: So from that crew, I felt imposed to take a half an hour.

743
00:37:16,123 --> 00:37:20,425
[SPEAKER_04]: I didn't think he was unhappy to quit then.

744
00:37:23,587 --> 00:37:24,968
[SPEAKER_04]: The living room was dark.

745
00:37:24,988 --> 00:37:27,769
[SPEAKER_04]: There were no lights on in there.

746
00:37:27,789 --> 00:37:30,570
[SPEAKER_04]: I thought we might take some.

747
00:37:30,811 --> 00:37:32,111
[SPEAKER_04]: We did take a couple pictures.

748
00:37:32,191 --> 00:37:34,692
[SPEAKER_04]: I hope they came out, but there wasn't much.

749
00:37:34,712 --> 00:37:37,574
[SPEAKER_04]: It was kind of gloomy in a way.

750
00:37:38,050 --> 00:37:40,112
[SPEAKER_00]: But he didn't have any pictures of himself.

751
00:37:40,232 --> 00:37:44,436
[SPEAKER_04]: He just had one of those cut-out postboard ones.

752
00:37:44,756 --> 00:37:45,957
[SPEAKER_00]: When he played at Oakland?

753
00:37:45,977 --> 00:37:49,681
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, it was standing up, which was over in the mantelpiece.

754
00:37:49,701 --> 00:37:50,562
[SPEAKER_04]: That was all he had.

755
00:37:50,582 --> 00:37:53,905
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, he wasn't up that long, was he?

756
00:37:53,925 --> 00:37:56,187
[SPEAKER_04]: No, he played quite a few years in the Coast League, though.

757
00:37:56,727 --> 00:37:59,330
[SPEAKER_04]: He had his career ran over 10 or 12 years.

758
00:38:00,330 --> 00:38:01,952
[SPEAKER_04]: But he was lost some time in the war.

759
00:38:02,032 --> 00:38:04,014
[SPEAKER_04]: I guess I had heard that.

760
00:38:04,935 --> 00:38:08,316
[SPEAKER_04]: He'd been gassed in the war, and this really affected his career.

761
00:38:08,937 --> 00:38:11,217
[SPEAKER_04]: Then he had a couple of injuries, too, at the time.

762
00:38:11,297 --> 00:38:15,379
[SPEAKER_04]: Somebody ran into him there and broke his hand.

763
00:38:16,680 --> 00:38:21,882
[SPEAKER_04]: So he had some misfortunes, which certainly affected his career.

764
00:38:23,322 --> 00:38:26,883
[SPEAKER_04]: Didn't, I don't know, didn't seem to have too much of a sense of humor.

765
00:38:26,903 --> 00:38:33,366
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know that I expected this, but it was pretty cut and dry, the whole business.

766
00:38:34,801 --> 00:38:40,502
[SPEAKER_04]: So I didn't really have any desire to drag it out much longer.

767
00:38:41,503 --> 00:38:43,763
[SPEAKER_04]: Covered the ground as best we could in half an hour.

768
00:38:43,783 --> 00:38:47,124
[SPEAKER_04]: That's probably the longest interview he's ever had, I suppose.

769
00:38:47,144 --> 00:38:48,544
[SPEAKER_04]: That's about the shortest one I've had.

770
00:38:49,224 --> 00:38:50,905
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think we've covered the ground on him.

771
00:38:51,725 --> 00:38:53,706
[SPEAKER_04]: We've got about 20 minutes to go on this side.

772
00:38:59,647 --> 00:39:03,908
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, after we left him, it was around 12, 15 or so, we drove into Sonoma.

773
00:39:04,768 --> 00:39:08,310
[SPEAKER_04]: which was Thornton Lee's hometown, whom we interviewed a couple of days ago.

774
00:39:09,490 --> 00:39:14,512
[SPEAKER_04]: No, a couple of weeks ago, I guess, before Palm Springs.

775
00:39:16,353 --> 00:39:31,860
[SPEAKER_04]: And we found Sonoma a very attractive town, quite historic, a plaza that looked like a real Spanish laid-out town, much like Santa Fe had been, with these low-story buildings circumventing the square.

776
00:39:32,688 --> 00:39:33,768
[SPEAKER_04]: Some historic buildings.

777
00:39:33,788 --> 00:39:44,690
[SPEAKER_04]: They have the monument where the Bear Flag Republic was raised back in 1846, announcing the independence of California, which was probably repudiated by the United States.

778
00:39:44,750 --> 00:39:49,331
[SPEAKER_04]: For a brief month or so, there was an independent state of California.

779
00:39:50,791 --> 00:39:51,712
[SPEAKER_04]: That's on the square.

780
00:39:52,692 --> 00:39:55,852
[SPEAKER_04]: The home of Yeho, and I never knew who he was.

781
00:39:55,892 --> 00:39:57,293
[SPEAKER_00]: The founders didn't know him.

782
00:39:59,368 --> 00:40:04,971
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was the one who was governor, or whoever, whatever it was, when they raised the bear flag.

783
00:40:07,752 --> 00:40:08,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I see.

784
00:40:09,873 --> 00:40:13,114
[SPEAKER_00]: And the mission there is the northernmost mission in the mission trail.

785
00:40:13,795 --> 00:40:15,055
[SPEAKER_04]: That would have been nice to see that mission.

786
00:40:15,075 --> 00:40:16,416
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm sorry we didn't.

787
00:40:16,576 --> 00:40:20,317
[SPEAKER_04]: But what happened was we got a little concerned about a place to stay.

788
00:40:21,238 --> 00:40:25,020
[SPEAKER_04]: And the other interviews, I tried to call Babe Pennelly and got all kinds of a run around there.

789
00:40:25,040 --> 00:40:25,820
[SPEAKER_04]: Never could find him.

790
00:40:27,044 --> 00:40:28,205
[SPEAKER_04]: It was along about 2 o'clock.

791
00:40:28,225 --> 00:40:33,268
[SPEAKER_04]: We decided to find a place to stay and then maybe see Sonora better, or completely.

792
00:40:33,288 --> 00:40:34,729
[SPEAKER_04]: They had a winery we wanted to go through.

793
00:40:35,490 --> 00:40:40,053
[SPEAKER_04]: But the one motel in town was taken, so we drove over here.

794
00:40:40,093 --> 00:40:42,935
[SPEAKER_04]: By the time we got here, we got all set up.

795
00:40:42,955 --> 00:40:44,536
[SPEAKER_04]: We just didn't feel like going back there.

796
00:40:44,837 --> 00:40:46,058
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything would have been closed in.

797
00:40:46,178 --> 00:40:51,061
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, well, we might have barely made it, but there probably would have been time for a tour of the winery.

798
00:40:51,081 --> 00:40:54,283
[SPEAKER_04]: And I don't know what else we could have seen.

799
00:40:56,202 --> 00:41:01,985
[SPEAKER_04]: So we probably should have done something else, but that's the way it worked out.

800
00:41:02,025 --> 00:41:04,747
[SPEAKER_04]: We didn't get the Penelli interview.

801
00:41:05,107 --> 00:41:09,869
[SPEAKER_04]: Mark Koenig, he's got his name in the phone book, but I don't know that we'll be able to see him.

802
00:41:09,889 --> 00:41:17,413
[SPEAKER_04]: We've got to get going home one of these days, and I think we're going to start tomorrow as we go farther north and west.

803
00:41:17,914 --> 00:41:20,155
[SPEAKER_04]: We keep getting farther away from home as we go home.

804
00:41:20,855 --> 00:41:22,616
[SPEAKER_04]: But we've got to go farther away to start back.

805
00:41:22,716 --> 00:41:23,757
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the only way we can do it.

806
00:41:26,219 --> 00:41:44,604
[SPEAKER_04]: So I guess that's about it on the Louis Guisto interview.

807
00:41:45,164 --> 00:41:53,286
[SPEAKER_04]: It wasn't the greatest interview, but it did satisfy a desire I'd long had to meet Louis Guisto.

808
00:41:55,290 --> 00:41:55,750
[SPEAKER_04]: Anything else?

809
00:41:56,350 --> 00:41:56,530
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

810
00:41:56,890 --> 00:41:58,411
[SPEAKER_04]: Tomorrow it's on the road up north.

811
00:41:58,471 --> 00:42:03,212
[SPEAKER_04]: And maybe we'll have a great interview with Joe Oscar, who's probably 88.

812
00:42:03,292 --> 00:42:04,152
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, God.

813
00:42:04,593 --> 00:42:05,293
[SPEAKER_04]: In Ferndale.

814
00:42:06,993 --> 00:42:09,114
[SPEAKER_04]: But he pitched in that long game.

815
00:42:09,594 --> 00:42:10,054
[SPEAKER_04]: We'll see.

816
00:42:10,074 --> 00:42:11,774
[SPEAKER_04]: But that's all for this time.

817
00:42:21,237 --> 00:42:22,637
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's a good while back.

818
00:42:22,857 --> 00:42:23,258
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

819
00:42:24,558 --> 00:42:25,078
[SPEAKER_04]: When was it?

820
00:42:27,467 --> 00:42:28,528
[SPEAKER_04]: You mean professionally?

821
00:42:28,668 --> 00:42:28,948
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

822
00:42:29,968 --> 00:42:31,029
[SPEAKER_04]: Minor leagues first.

823
00:42:31,089 --> 00:42:31,749
[SPEAKER_02]: Minor leagues?

824
00:42:32,930 --> 00:42:39,293
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I broke in in 1914 in minor league.

825
00:42:41,134 --> 00:42:43,715
[SPEAKER_02]: And then I played there for... Where'd you play?

826
00:42:49,078 --> 00:42:52,200
[SPEAKER_02]: An old Central League.

827
00:42:52,460 --> 00:42:52,820
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh yeah?

828
00:42:53,300 --> 00:42:54,301
[SPEAKER_04]: Not far from here, huh?

829
00:42:54,441 --> 00:42:56,802
[SPEAKER_02]: No, just right over here at Springfield.

830
00:42:58,557 --> 00:43:03,661
[SPEAKER_02]: Dayton, Springfield, Wheeling, Evansville, they were in that league then.

831
00:43:05,383 --> 00:43:23,819
[SPEAKER_02]: And then I went from there up to, well I didn't break in there, I upgraded up at Saginaw, the old Southern Michigan League.

832
00:43:24,139 --> 00:43:25,160
[SPEAKER_02]: Ducky Holmes, he was

833
00:43:26,992 --> 00:43:29,513
[SPEAKER_02]: the manager of the Saginaw team.

834
00:43:30,573 --> 00:43:31,994
[SPEAKER_02]: So I went up there with him.

835
00:43:33,134 --> 00:43:39,516
[SPEAKER_02]: And then after that, after they quit up there, I went over to Detroit.

